To merge arrays in JavaScript without duplicates, the most efficient approach combines the spread operator with a Set. Here is a comprehensive example:

```javascript
const arr1 = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
const arr2 = [4, 5, 6, 7, 8];

// Method 1: Set with spread (recommended for primitives)
const merged = [...new Set([...arr1, ...arr2])];
// Result: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]

// Method 2: Using Array.from
const merged2 = Array.from(new Set([...arr1, ...arr2]));

// Method 3: filter + indexOf (slower, O(n^2))
const all = [...arr1, ...arr2];
const merged3 = all.filter((item, index) => all.indexOf(item) === index);

// Method 4: For object arrays deduplicated by property
const users1 = [{id: 1, name: 'Alice'}, {id: 2, name: 'Bob'}];
const users2 = [{id: 2, name: 'Bob'}, {id: 3, name: 'Charlie'}];
const mergedUsers = [
  ...new Map(
    [...users1, ...users2].map(u => [u.id, u])
  ).values()
];
// Result: [{id: 1, name: 'Alice'}, {id: 2, name: 'Bob'}, {id: 3, name: 'Charlie'}]

// Method 5: reduce with a Set accumulator
const merged5 = [...arr1, ...arr2].reduce((acc, item) => {
  return acc.has(item) ? acc : acc.add(item);
}, new Set());
// Convert to array: [...merged5]
```

The Set approach works for primitive values (numbers, strings, booleans, null, undefined, symbols) because Set uses `SameValueZero` equality. For objects, Set uses reference equality, so two objects with identical properties are considered different unless they are the same reference. For property-based deduplication of objects, the Map technique shown above is the best approach. The filter+indexOf method works for primitives but is O(n^2) time complexity, making it impractical for large arrays. When preserving order, the Set method keeps the first occurrence of each value, which is usually the desired behavior.

The `concat()` method in JavaScript merges two or more arrays into a new array without modifying the original arrays. Here are multiple examples:

```javascript
// Basic concatenation
const letters = ['a', 'b', 'c'];
const numbers = [1, 2, 3];
const combined = letters.concat(numbers);
// ['a', 'b', 'c', 1, 2, 3]

// Concatenating multiple arrays
const arr1 = [1, 2];
const arr2 = [3, 4];
const arr3 = [5, 6];
const all = arr1.concat(arr2, arr3);
// [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]

// Concatenating values (not just arrays)
const result = [1, 2].concat(3, 4, [5, 6]);
// [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]

// Chaining concat
const chained = [1].concat([2]).concat([3]).concat([4]);
// [1, 2, 3, 4]

// Using concat to shallow copy an array
const original = [1, 2, {a: 1}];
const copy = [].concat(original);
// or: const copy = original.concat();
copy[2].a = 99;
console.log(original[2].a); // 99 — shallow copy

// Merging and filtering in one chain
const filtered = letters
  .concat(numbers)
  .filter(item => typeof item === 'number');
// [1, 2, 3]

// Using concat with empty arrays
const empty = [].concat([1, 2], [], [3]);
// [1, 2, 3]
```

Key behaviors of `concat()`: it returns a new array, does not modify the originals, only flattens one level of array arguments (if you pass a nested array like `[[1,2]]`, it adds it as a single element), and preserves the order of elements from each source. The method works in all JavaScript environments, including very old browsers. While the spread operator is more modern, `concat()` remains useful when you need to merge values that may or may not be arrays (since it can accept both arrays and individual values).

Combining arrays in JavaScript can be done with several methods depending on whether you want a new array, in-place modification, deduplication, or transformation. Here are the main approaches:

```javascript
const a = ['apple', 'banana'];
const b = ['cherry', 'date'];

// 1. Spread operator (new array)
const combo = [...a, ...b];
// ['apple', 'banana', 'cherry', 'date']

// 2. Concat method (new array)
const combo2 = a.concat(b);

// 3. Push with spread (in-place modification)
a.push(...b);
// a is now ['apple', 'banana', 'cherry', 'date']

// 4. Unshift with spread (prepend)
a.unshift(...['zucchini']);

// 5. Splice for insertion at index
const target = [1, 5, 6];
target.splice(1, 0, ...[2, 3, 4]);
// target is now [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]

// 6. Combining and deduplicating
const x = [1, 2, 3];
const y = [2, 3, 4];
const unique = [...new Set([...x, ...y])];
// [1, 2, 3, 4]

// 7. Combining with transformation (map)
const doubled = [...a.map(f => f.toUpperCase()), ...b];

// 8. Conditional combination
const includeB = true;
const items = [
  ...a,
  ...(includeB ? b : []),
];

// 9. Using push.apply (for very large arrays, older JS)
const big = [];
Array.prototype.push.apply(big, a);
Array.prototype.push.apply(big, b);

// 10. Reduce (functional style)
const combined = [a, b].reduce((acc, arr) => [...acc, ...arr], []);
```

The spread operator (`...`) is the most readable and is the standard in modern JavaScript. Use `concat()` when you need compatibility with older environments or when method chaining. Use `push(...)` for in-place merging. For removing duplicates, always wrap with `new Set()`. For performance-critical code with extremely large arrays, benchmark the options because spread and concat can have different performance characteristics in different engines.

The JavaScript spread operator (`...`) provides a concise syntax for merging arrays. It unpacks elements from an iterable into individual elements within a new array. Here are detailed merge patterns:

```javascript
const team1 = ['Alice', 'Bob'];
const team2 = ['Charlie', 'Diana'];

// Basic merge
const all = [...team1, ...team2];
// ['Alice', 'Bob', 'Charlie', 'Diana']

// Merge with inline elements
const extended = ['Start', ...team1, ...team2, 'End'];
// ['Start', 'Alice', 'Bob', 'Charlie', 'Diana', 'End']

// Conditional merge
const showTeam2 = true;
const roster = [
  ...team1,
  ...(showTeam2 ? team2 : []),
];

// Merge multiple arrays
const combined = [...team1, ...team2, ...['Eve', 'Frank']];

// Spread works with any iterable
const mySet = new Set([1, 2, 3]);
const myMap = new Map([['a', 1], ['b', 2]]);
const merged = [...mySet, ...myMap.keys()];
// [1, 2, 3, 'a', 'b']

// In-place push with spread
const existing = [1, 2];
existing.push(...[3, 4, 5]);
// existing is now [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]

// Creating a copy before merge
const original = [1, 2, 3];
const mergedCopy = [...original, 4, 5];
// original is unchanged: [1, 2, 3]

// Combining spread with other methods
const nums1 = [1, 2, 3];
const nums2 = [4, 5, 6];
const transformed = [...nums1, ...nums2.map(n => n * 10)];
// [1, 2, 3, 40, 50, 60]

// Spread in function arguments (not array merge but related)
const max = Math.max(...nums1, ...nums2);
```

The spread operator creates a shallow copy, so nested objects and arrays remain referenced. It only works for iterable objects (arrays, strings, Sets, Maps, NodeLists). It cannot be used to spread objects directly into arrays (use object spread `{...obj}` for objects). The spread operator is transpiled by Babel for older browser support. Performance-wise, spread is comparable to concat for most use cases but can be slower for extremely large arrays because it explicitly iterates each element. Nevertheless, spread is the most readable and commonly used approach for array merging in modern JavaScript development.

JavaScript provides a rich set of methods for array manipulation including merging, adding, removing, transforming, and filtering elements. Here is a comprehensive reference focused on common operations:

```javascript
const arr = [1, 2, 3];
const more = [4, 5, 6];

// ── Adding elements ──
arr.push(...more);      // Append (in-place)
arr.unshift(0);          // Prepend (in-place)
const newArr = [0, ...arr, ...more, 7]; // New array via spread

// ── Merging ──
const merged = arr.concat(more);         // Traditional
const merged2 = [...arr, ...more];        // Modern
const merged3 = [].concat(arr, more, [7]); // Chained

// ── Removing elements ──
const last = arr.pop();                  // Remove last
const first = arr.shift();               // Remove first
const removed = arr.splice(1, 2);        // Remove by index
const filtered = arr.filter(n => n > 2); // Remove by condition

// ── Transforming ──
const doubled = arr.map(n => n * 2);
const reversed = [...arr].reverse();
const sorted = [...arr].sort((a, b) => a - b);

// ── Combining operations ──
const result = arr
  .concat(more)
  .filter(n => n % 2 === 0)
  .map(n => n * 10);

// ── Unique merge ──
const unique = [...new Set([...arr, ...more])];

// ── Immutable updates (React style) ──
const state = [1, 2, 3];
// Add:      [...state, 4]
// Remove:   state.filter(x => x !== 2)
// Replace:  state.map(x => x === 2 ? 99 : x)
// Prepend:  [0, ...state]
// Insert:   [...state.slice(0, 1), 1.5, ...state.slice(1)]
```

Key principles: prefer immutable methods (spread, concat, map, filter, slice) over mutable ones (push, splice, sort, reverse) when you want to avoid side effects. Modern JavaScript development, especially with React, strongly favors immutability. For performance, be aware that spread and concat are O(n) and creating new arrays — fine for most use cases but heavy within tight loops. The method chaining pattern (filter → map → reduce) is idiomatic functional JavaScript.

Joining two arrays in JavaScript creates a new array containing all elements from both source arrays. The primary methods are the spread operator and `concat()`. Here are practical examples:

```javascript
const fruits = ['apple', 'banana'];
const berries = ['blueberry', 'raspberry'];

// Spread operator (modern)
const fruitBowl = [...fruits, ...berries];
// ['apple', 'banana', 'blueberry', 'raspberry']

// concat() method
const fruitBowl2 = fruits.concat(berries);
// ['apple', 'banana', 'blueberry', 'raspberry']

// Join without duplicates
const a = ['x', 'y', 'z'];
const b = ['y', 'z', 'w'];
const joined = [...new Set([...a, ...b])];
// ['x', 'y', 'z', 'w']

// Join in-place (modifies first array)
fruits.push(...berries);
// fruits is now ['apple', 'banana', 'blueberry', 'raspberry']

// Join at a specific position (immutable)
const insertAt = 1;
const result = [
  ...fruits.slice(0, insertAt),
  ...['new item'],
  ...fruits.slice(insertAt)
];

// Conditional join
const shouldAdd = true;
const items = [
  'base',
  ...(shouldAdd ? ['extra'] : []),
];

// Using Array.prototype.push.apply (older approach, non-spread)
const target = [1, 2];
Array.prototype.push.apply(target, [3, 4]);
// target is [1, 2, 3, 4]
```

Note the difference between joining arrays (combining them into one larger array) and the `.join()` string method, which concatenates array elements into a string. Joining arrays is a frequent operation in state management, data processing pipelines, and when combining API response data. For most modern codebases, the spread operator is the standard choice due to its readability and flexibility.

The `reduce()` method provides a functional programming approach to merging arrays. While less common than spread or concat for simple merges, reduce is powerful for merge logic with conditional filtering or transformation. Here are examples:

```javascript
const arrays = [[1, 2], [3, 4], [5, 6]];

// Basic merging of multiple arrays
const merged = arrays.reduce((acc, curr) => [...acc, ...curr], []);
// [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]

// Using concat inside reduce (more efficient for many arrays)
const merged2 = arrays.reduce((acc, curr) => acc.concat(curr), []);

// Merge and filter out duplicates
const a = [1, 2, 3, 4];
const b = [3, 4, 5, 6];
const unique = [a, b].reduce((acc, arr) => {
  arr.forEach(item => {
    if (!acc.includes(item)) acc.push(item);
  });
  return acc;
}, []);
// [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]

// Better: reduce with Set for deduplication
const deduped = [...[a, b].reduce((set, arr) => {
  arr.forEach(item => set.add(item));
  return set;
}, new Set())];

// Merge with transformation
const mergedTransformed = arrays.reduce((acc, arr) => [
  ...acc,
  ...arr.map(n => n * 2)
], []);
// [2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12]

// Merge objects by key
const users1 = [{id: 1, name: 'Alice'}, {id: 2, name: 'Bob'}];
const users2 = [{id: 2, name: 'Bob Updated'}, {id: 3, name: 'Charlie'}];
const mergedUsers = [users1, users2].reduce((map, users) => {
  users.forEach(u => map.set(u.id, u));
  return map;
}, new Map());
const result = [...mergedUsers.values()];
// [{id: 1, name: 'Alice'}, {id: 2, name: 'Bob Updated'}, {id: 3, name: 'Charlie'}]

// Merge with conditional inclusion
const mergedFiltered = arrays.reduce((acc, arr) => [
  ...acc,
  ...arr.filter(n => n > 3)
], []);
// [4, 5, 6]
```

Reduce is especially useful when you need to merge many arrays with custom logic. However, for simple merges, the spread operator or concat with `[].concat(...arrays)` is more readable. The performance overhead of spreading inside reduce (creating intermediate arrays at each step) means for very large datasets, `push.apply` inside reduce would be faster: `arrays.reduce((acc, arr) => { acc.push(...arr); return acc; }, [])`.

Merging arrays of objects in JavaScript requires special consideration because simple concatenation won't remove duplicate objects. Here are the main patterns:

```javascript
const team1 = [
  { id: 1, name: 'Alice', role: 'dev' },
  { id: 2, name: 'Bob', role: 'design' },
];
const team2 = [
  { id: 2, name: 'Bob', role: 'dev' },  // Updated role
  { id: 3, name: 'Charlie', role: 'pm' },
];

// 1. Simple merge (keeps duplicates)
const simple = [...team1, ...team2];
// 4 objects, Bob appears twice

// 2. Merge and deduplicate by id (last wins — updates Bob's role)
const byId = [...new Map(
  [...team1, ...team2].map(obj => [obj.id, obj])
).values()];
// [{id:1, name:'Alice', role:'dev'}, {id:2, name:'Bob', role:'dev'}, {id:3, name:'Charlie', role:'pm'}]

// 3. Merge and deduplicate by id (first wins — keeps Bob's original)
const firstWin = [...new Map(
  [...team2, ...team1].map(obj => [obj.id, obj])
).values()];

// 4. Merge with deep merge of properties (using Lodash)
// import _ from 'lodash';
// const deepMerged = _.merge(_.keyBy(team1, 'id'), _.keyBy(team2, 'id'));
// const result = Object.values(deepMerged);

// 5. Merge with manual update logic
const merged = [...team1];
const map = new Map(merged.map(u => [u.id, u]));
team2.forEach(obj => {
  if (map.has(obj.id)) {
    Object.assign(map.get(obj.id), obj);  // Merge properties
  } else {
    merged.push(obj);
  }
});

// 6. Filter merge (only add new objects)
const existingIds = new Set(team1.map(u => u.id));
const filteredMerge = [
  ...team1,
  ...team2.filter(obj => !existingIds.has(obj.id)),
];

// 7. Merge with a composite key
const keyed = [...new Map(
  [...team1, ...team2].map(obj => [`${obj.id}-${obj.role}`, obj])
).values()];
```

The Map-based deduplication (method 2) is the most common and efficient approach for merging arrays of objects by a unique key. It has O(n) time complexity, preserves insertion order, and gives you control over whether first or last occurrence wins. For simple concatenation without deduplication, the spread operator works fine.

The `flat()` method in JavaScript can be used to merge multiple arrays into one when they are nested inside a parent array. This is particularly useful when you have an array of arrays and want to flatten them into a single array. Here are examples:

```javascript
const arrays = [[1, 2], [3, 4], [5, 6]];

// flat() with depth 1 merges nested arrays into one
const merged = arrays.flat();
// [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]

// This is equivalent to:
const merged2 = [].concat(...arrays);
// or: const merged3 = arrays.reduce((acc, arr) => [...acc, ...arr], []);

// flat() works with deeper nesting
const deepArrays = [[1, [2, 3]], [[4, 5], 6]];
const flatOnce = deepArrays.flat();   // [1, [2, 3], [4, 5], 6]
const flatDeep = deepArrays.flat(2);  // [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]

// flat() with Infinity for arbitrary depth
const veryDeep = [1, [2, [3, [4]]]];
const fullyFlat = veryDeep.flat(Infinity);
// [1, 2, 3, 4]

// Combining map and flat using flatMap
const strings = ['hello world', 'foo bar'];
const words = strings.flatMap(s => s.split(' '));
// ['hello', 'world', 'foo', 'bar']

// flatMap is equivalent to: arr.map(...).flat()

// Merging arrays of arrays with duplicate removal
const withDupes = [[1, 2], [2, 3], [3, 4]];
const unique = [...new Set(withDupes.flat())];
// [1, 2, 3, 4]

// Conditional merge using flatMap
const items = [1, 2, 3, 4];
const expanded = items.flatMap(n => n % 2 === 0 ? [n, n * 10] : [n]);
// [1, 2, 20, 3, 4, 40]

// Replacing filter + map with flatMap
const result = items.flatMap(n => n > 2 ? [n * 2] : []);
// [6, 8]
```

The `flat()` method is part of ES2019 and is well-supported in modern browsers and Node.js 11+. For older environments, a polyfill is needed or use `[].concat(...arrays)` as an alternative. `flatMap()` is particularly powerful because it allows you to map each element to zero, one, or multiple output elements, making it useful for both merging and transforming in a single pass.
